WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.047
A symptom cannot tell a clinician which immune

00:00:04.047 --> 00:00:07.194
mechanism produced it. Tissue, timing and clinical

00:00:07.194 --> 00:00:08.993
context complicate the answer.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.204
Abdominal illness can look similar across immune,

00:00:19.204 --> 00:00:21.886
inflammatory and infectious processes. Clinical

00:00:21.886 --> 00:00:24.567
appearance alone may be insufficient.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.415
Diagnostic interpretation can involve biomarkers,

00:00:33.415 --> 00:00:38.158
endoscopy and tissue findings, with different roles and

00:00:38.158 --> 00:00:40.530
limitations in distinct diseases.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.508
Part Two surveys emerging diagnostic techniques and

00:00:49.508 --> 00:00:52.407
treatments for autoimmune gastrointestinal conditions

00:00:52.407 --> 00:00:55.305
in a changing evidence landscape.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.798
Therapeutic approaches follow disease mechanisms, but

00:01:03.798 --> 00:01:07.703
treatment evidence differs by condition, study design

00:01:07.703 --> 00:01:09.377
and patient population.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.703
Diagnostic tools are useful when interpreted together.

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:23.815
An illustration of a microscope is not an image of a

00:01:23.815 --> 00:01:25.209
patient's diagnostic specimen.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.860
A new assay or biologic may improve the available

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.289
choices without solving every diagnostic or therapeutic

00:01:39.289 --> 00:01:39.779
question.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:50.366
The literature review maps available approaches; it is

00:01:50.366 --> 00:01:54.281
not a replacement for updated guidelines or an

00:01:54.281 --> 00:01:55.260
individual diagnosis.
